    "case_id": "mxh011",
    "case_summary": "A 5-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department with complaints of a sore throat and difficulty breathing. His mother reports that the symptoms began the previous night and have worsened rapidly. The child is normally healthy, up to date on all childhood vaccinations, and takes a daily multivitamin. His temperature is 103°F (39.4°C), blood pressure is 100/64 mmHg, pulse is 155/min, respirations are 29/min, and oxygen saturation is 95% on room air. On examination, he appears ill, is drooling, and leans forward to breathe. He is nonverbal, visibly uncomfortable, and does not cooperate with an oral examination. What is the most likely infectious cause of this patient’s symptoms?\n\nOptions:\nA. Haemophilus influenzae\nB. Streptococcus pneumoniae\nC. Parainfluenza virus\nD. Staphylococcus aureus\nE. Group A Streptococcus\nF. Adenovirus\nG. Mycoplasma pneumoniae\nH. Epstein-Barr virus\nI. Corynebacterium diphtheriae\nJ. Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)\n",
